-- title:
--   Lemma 2.1 — finite-domain Nullstellensatz for $\langle g_D(x), h_R(s)\rangle$
-- statement:
--   Let $P$ be a CSP($\Gamma$) instance over a nonempty finite domain $D\subseteq\mathbb Q$, and let $I(P)=\langle g_D(x)\ (x\in X),\ h_R(s)\ (\langle s,R\rangle\in C)\rangle$ be the ideal generated by its polynomial encoding. Then:
--
--   1. (weak Nullstellensatz) $\mathrm{Sol}(P)=\emptyset \iff 1\in I(P)\iff I(P)=\mathbb Q[X]$;
--   2. (strong Nullstellensatz) for every $f_0\in\mathbb Q[X]$,
--   $$
--   f_0\in I(P)\iff f_0(\varphi)=0\ \text{ for every }\varphi\in\mathrm{Sol}(P).
--   $$
--
--   So the generated ideal coincides with the vanishing ideal of the solutions, which is the ideal $\mathrm{IMP}_d(\Gamma)$ asks about.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - reading: I(P) in Lemma 2.1 (defined on p.3 as the vanishing ideal, and displayed on p.11 as ⟨g_D(x), h_R(s)⟩) → genIdeal P, the ideal generated by the p.11 display; reason: with the vanishing ideal the strong Nullstellensatz would be a tautology; the lemma asserts the correspondence for the generated ideal
--   - encoding: an instance P = (X, D, C) with variables x_1, …, x_n and a set C of constraints → Instance: variables Fin n and a list of constraints (relation index, scope Fin r → Fin n); reason: Lean convention; order and repetition of constraints change neither Sol(P) nor I(P)
-- source:
--   Rafiey–Soleymani–Jaillet, A Dichotomy Theorem for CSP-Based Ideal Membership, STOC 2027 submission manuscript (held version, sha256 c7140009133170c893c7b31cf2279f3dda0264d867825a8beb7cc6963f50f109), Lemma 2.1, p.11 (PDF 11), with the generator display on the same page

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_CSPIdealMem_GenIdeal

namespace CSPIdealMem

/-- Rafiey–Soleymani–Jaillet, *A Dichotomy Theorem for CSP-Based Ideal Membership*, STOC 2027
submission manuscript (held version, sha256 c7140009…f50f109), Lemma 2.1 (finite-domain Nullstellensatz), p.11 (PDF 11), for the ideal
`I(P) = ⟨g_D(x) (x ∈ X), h_R(s) (⟨s, R⟩ ∈ C)⟩` displayed on the same page.

For every instance `P` over a nonempty finite `D ⊆ ℚ`: (1) `Sol(P) = ∅ ⟺ 1 ∈ I(P) ⟺ I(P) = ℚ[X]`;
(2) `f₀ ∈ I(P) ⟺ f₀(φ) = 0` for every `φ ∈ Sol(P)`.

**Formalization Note.** `I(P)` here is the generated ideal `genIdeal P` (in the lemma the CSP
ideal is the ideal of the p.11 display); clause (2) says it equals the vanishing ideal of p.3. -/
theorem lemma_2_1 (D : Finset ℚ) (hD : D.Nonempty) (Γ : ConstraintLanguage D) (P : Instance Γ) :
    (Sol P = ∅ ↔ (1 : MvPolynomial (Fin P.n) ℚ) ∈ genIdeal P) ∧
    ((1 : MvPolynomial (Fin P.n) ℚ) ∈ genIdeal P ↔ genIdeal P = ⊤) ∧
    ∀ f : MvPolynomial (Fin P.n) ℚ,
      f ∈ genIdeal P ↔ ∀ φ ∈ Sol P, MvPolynomial.eval (fun x => ((φ x : D) : ℚ)) f = 0 := by sorry

end CSPIdealMem
